{text-align:center}

( Looking first at the timelines of publications in "replication crisis" (left) and "open science" (rights) subjects, it is visible that they have been emerging, hot controversies in the past decade. Reproducibility has been one of the informal foundations for our trust in science, along with a few others like falsifiability and generalizability. Psychology, not being a science typically contesting these epistemologies, came under scrutiny in the Open Science Foundation's first large-scale Reproducibility Project between 2013-2015 following a few suspicious publications. The concern quickly escalated to policy levels, with agreements for promoting Open Science as a means to proceed with some academic housekeeping made both in the EU, the US, and globally.

 My first query into academic articles on Scopus therefore combined the two involved terms ("replication crisis" or "reproducibility crisis"), and revealed three notably distinct discourses, one about relevant statistical methods (eg. "p-hacking"), one about medical sciences, and one about Open Science initiatives. This lead me to follow up with a second query, this time for "open science", which returned 9369 articles, providing robust input for the exponential timeline. The semantic analysis resulted in a dense network, so I added a conjunction of "epistemology" to my query, yielding the network on the right. The replication crisis and medical science reemerged as a distinct cluster, but "Open science" split a plurality of overlapping topics, with fine details about methodological, cultural and educational concerns. The central "bridging" area in both networks touch on concerns particular to the social sciences, all showing great potential for sentiment analysis, with mixed senses of constructive concepts, and strong criticism.)#span{display:inline-block;max-width:500px;text-align:justify}

